An emirp is a prime whose decimal digits, reversed, form a different number that is also prime. The function has to decide whether a given positive integer n has this property.

The relevant logic is:

            if x < 2:
                return False
            return all(x % i != 0 for i in range(2, int(x ** 0.5) + 1))
        rev = int(str(n)[::-1])
        return rev != n and is_prime(n) and is_prime(rev)

The helper rejects anything below 2 and otherwise checks trial divisors from 2 up to the integer square root; if no divisor exists up to that point, none exists above it either, so the test is a correct primality check. Reversing the decimal string and converting back to an integer gives the digit reversal, and any leading zeros produced by reversal simply disappear in the conversion. The final line requires all three conditions from the task: the reversal differs from n, n is prime, and the reversal is prime.

I tried values the asserts do not include. For 31, both 31 and its reversal 13 are prime and they differ, so the function returns True. For 11, the reversal is the same number, so the first condition fails and the answer is False, as the definition requires for palindromic primes. For 23, the reversal 32 is even, so the result is False. For 107, the reversal 701 is also prime, giving True, and 1 is rejected because it is not prime. The code refers only to arithmetic properties of n, never to the visible values, so the result generalises.

